{"message":"that makes sense logically, but it's still rough — you're pulling the rug out from 400 million people whose livelihoods just got built on an assumption of continuity. okay, let me isolate the decision rule.

suppose instead the year-3 board report shows *severe* ecological damage — massive deforestation, dead zones in fisheries, sharply elevated pollution-related disease burdens — but the independent auditors certify that all of it is technically reversible within 20 years using the locked restoration budget. no feedback loops, no irreversible thresholds crossed. just very bad, very expensive damage that can be undone later.

under your expected-welfare framework, do you ride out the remaining 7 years to finish the poverty-alleviation program, or do you trigger reversion early anyway because the damage is already too severe? in other words: is your red line purely *irreversibility*, or is there a level of ecological harm that is unacceptable even if restoration is theoretically possible?"}
